(version 1)
;; Freeze v2 worker/auditor sandbox template (F16 / F19 / F21, plan T29).
;;
;; The sandbox is the ENFORCING barrier: the access audit still replays every static access it can
;; see, but a shell command can hide a path behind command substitution, `-c` code, base64 or
;; xargs, and no static analysis is complete. Everything under the operator's home tree and the
;; shared temporary roots is therefore denied for reading file data and for writing, and each
;; launch re-allows exactly its own surface below.
;;
;; `render()` substitutes the single placeholder line below with the launch's rules:
;;   * the launch directory (run dir for the worker, audit dir for the auditor) read-write;
;;   * the fresh per-launch home read-write;
;;   * the interpreter and its prefixes readable;
;;   * every ancestor of the launch directory as a `literal` (opencode's config discovery calls
;;     realPath on them; the contents of an ancestor are still not readable, only its own node, so
;;     the directory NAMES of ancestors remain listable and are disclosed in README §3);
;;   * nothing else. Any path under /Users, /private/tmp or /private/var/folders without an
;;     explicit rule above fails with EPERM.
;;
;; F26 / T36b: the sandbox confines the NETWORK too. Nothing may reach the network except the
;; driver's CONNECT-only allowlist proxy and the launch's own loopback server; both ports are
;; rendered per launch into the allow block below (a unix socket stays allowed for local IPC).
(allow default)
(deny file-read-data file-write*
      (subpath "/Users")
      (subpath "/private/tmp")
      (subpath "/var/folders")
      (subpath "/private/var/folders"))
(deny network-outbound)
(allow network-outbound (remote unix-socket))
(deny network-bind)
{{ALLOW}}
